  "description": "Estimate pi by evaluating the proportion of uniform 2D unit-square coordinates that land inside a quadrant of radius 1.\n\nMonte Carlo methods approximate intractable high-dimensional integrals by taking empirical sample averages over random variables.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\pi \\approx 4 \\times \\frac{N_{\\text{inside}}}{N_{\\text{total}}}, \\quad x^2 + y^2 \\le 1\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Evaluate Euclidean distance condition x^2 + y^2 <= 1 for all sampled coordinate pairs\n2. Count number of sampled points landing inside the quarter unit circle\n3. Multiply proportion by 4 to estimate circular area pi\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'total_samples', 'inside_circle', and 'pi_estimate'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Monte Carlo Pi Estimation\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world data is inherently noisy and uncertain. Probability theory and statistics provide principled tools to quantify that uncertainty, model how data is generated, and evaluate confidence in empirical results. **Monte Carlo Pi Estimation** evaluates probability distributions or statistical tests to summarize sample characteristics.\n\nMonte Carlo methods approximate intractable high-dimensional integrals by taking empirical sample averages over random variables. In this problem, the goal is to: estimate pi by evaluating the proportion of uniform 2d unit-square coordinates that land inside a quadrant of radius 1.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\pi \\approx 4 \\times \\frac{N_{\\text{inside}}}{N_{\\text{total}}}, \\quad x^2 + y^2 \\le 1\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Evaluate Euclidean distance condition x^2 + y^2 <= 1 for all sampled coordinate pairs\n2. **Phase 2**: Count number of sampled points landing inside the quarter unit circle\n3. **Phase 3**: Multiply proportion by 4 to estimate circular area pi\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Statistical Significance**: Machine learning A/B testing and model evaluation rely on p-values and confidence intervals to ensure observed improvements are not just random noise.\n* **Tail Probabilities & Underflow**: In deep generative models or Bayesian inference, computing log-probabilities prevents underflow when dealing with very small probability values in distribution tails.\n* **Moments & Distributions**: Examining mean, variance, skewness, and kurtosis helps data practitioners detect outliers and choose appropriate normalization strategies.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: points = [[0.5, 0.5], [0.1, 0.1], [0.9, 0.9], [0.2, 0.8]]\n\nOutput: {\"total_samples\": 4, \"inside_circle\": 3, \"pi_estimate\": 3.0}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated probstat_monte_carlo_pi under standard test conditions."
  ],
